one of the philosophers who many people  don't generally include is very  important in the Canon but who I find  very important in my own work I use him  a lot not only in terms of understanding  philosophical movements in an academic  sense but also in a very practical way  in my coaching and and work with with  individuals is Marcus Tullius Cicero and  he's somebody who has grown on me over  the years I think that he is not  sufficiently appreciated and that has  its roots I'll talk a little bit where  that came from you know it has its roots  in I think the 19th century and and how  philosophy and its history have been  looked at many people think of Cicero as  kind of a you know secondary derivative  thinker he's not as important to say  Plato Aristotle or or indeed the late  Stoics or Epicurus but I actually think  that he deserves to be placed up there  at the you know top level I think that  in addition to doing a lot of great work  as a translator and as a person  interpreting reinterpreting classic  philosophy he has some interesting ideas  of his own as well and I find myself  particularly attracted to him you know  at this stage in my life and career  because of his eclecticism and the way  in which he has articulated though how  what kind of eclectic he is and I talked  about that in another video so I will  come back to that but let me talk first  about how myself I got into Cicero and  developed a greater appreciation for him  over time so I actually have one of the  volumes that I first read by Cicero this  isn't the actual volume the actual  volume was in the Southern Illinois  University Library but this is the Loeb  series and as I was working on my Latin  you know and essentially doing it on my  own there's a whole story there you can  hear about in one of my other videos I  got to the point where I realized that I  needed to start reading other  reading authors not just reading little  snippets of them and so I thought well  you know Cicero he's an important guy  let me go to the library and I'm looking  through his different works and I saw  this one the academics and the academics  is a dialogue between several different  representatives of different  philosophical schools and I thought that  sounds really it sounds really cool I'll  check that out  Cicero is very tough to read in Latin  still is for me today although my level  is much higher than it was then and it  took me going through the semester took  me about the entire semester to get my  way through ten pages and these are not  you know particularly long pages I  notice ten pages where Latin is on one  side English on the other so you can  actually do some points of comparison it  took me a long time to get through that  and I was like boy maybe I bit off more  than I can chew I'll come back to Cicero  later on but I read the work and I was  really impressed with the fact that  Cicero could take a lot of people's  ideas and represent them in ways that I  knew were because I'd read other things  about you know Stoics Aristotelians  academic sceptics you know Epicureans  we're largely faithful to what they had  to say and he was also giving us  interesting background and reflections  on the Roman situation and in terms of  culture and the backstory so that was my  real introduction to Cicero and I  continued reading his works off and on  he wasn't somebody who we had to read in  our classes or in the massive reading  list for our comps and prelims but he  was somebody who I was reading and then  when I had the opportunity to start you  know teaching ancient philosophy first  at Indiana State Prison I taught that  class twice and then you know in other  other venues and also you know to  smuggle stuff into my intro classes I  started teaching Cicero's texts and you  can actually see videos where  I'm looking at his dialogue on  friendship or on the nature of the gods  I think those are the main ones that I  did but there's a lot of other great  works I actually brought several of them  here that I love going back to over and  over again Cicero's on duties which is  representing stoic philosophy the de  finibus are on the ends where he's got a  great dialogue going on between  representatives of several different  philosophical schools and even the  people who aren't being represented  directly they're being discussed so  Aristotelian Oh classic politeness as  opposed to academic platanus are being  represented there and so what I really I  mean this is a great jumping-off point  what I really like about Cicero is that  his dialogues are genuine you know  exchanges of view between people and and  it's guaranteed usually that the  epicurean is gonna lose Cicero is not  always completely fair to the Epicureans  representing them at their strongest  points but he does provide us with so  much you know great background and sort  of exposition of what the schools think  it turns out that that becomes  incredibly valuable for us given how  much of literature that we've lost from  classical antiquity so we know a lot  about the Stoics in part through Cicero  who is not himself a a stoic but who had  studied under Stoics who engaged with  them pretty routinely even fought side  by side with him if you think about you  know Cato in the Civil Wars and he is  good at at putting across what their  position is and then going into the  minutiae and explaining it if he hadn't  done that we would be at a severe  disadvantage we would also be at a  severe disadvantage in terms of the  Epicureans as well and we learn a lot  about the Aristotelian 's of his time  and how they saw you know the interplay  between say philosophy and rhetoric or  any of these these sorts of things as  well one of the other facets of Cicero's  work that I particularly admire and  enjoy is the fact that he is he's a  practical philosopher he was engaged in  political life in a number of different  ways in part through his arguing cases  as a jurist right and so he understands  that rhetoric is not only useful for its  own sake or interesting for its own sake  but that it has this a very important  social implication in terms of a house  you know how the laws are applied or not  applied he actually goes so far as to  write a thing about electioneering which  is quite interesting but regarded by  many as being very cynical in the lower  case C not not the upper case C which  was a actual philosophical school and  and what I see in Cicero is his capacity  to call out the practical implications  of schools like say the playtest one  that we might see as very impractical  and to drive this stuff home to talk  about well what would be the effects of  this he he's not the kind of person who  thinks that philosophers ought to simply  engage in their own leisure that they  they should be politically engaged and  involved especially if they're you know  in situations where they they could  exercise some power for good or for bad  he saw his own writing project and this  is another important point from where I  sit he saw his own project of writing  all these books as a massive and major  contribution to Latin culture in a way  he you might say that he occupies a  similar role to oh a theist coming  centuries later at the beginning of what  we call the dark ages  Boethius had decided to translate all of  Plato and Aristotle into Latin from from  Greek he didn't succeed in that because  he was executed before that but but  Cicero talks about taking all of the the  wisdom of the Greek philosophers and  even orders and sort of putting it into  a Latin idiom and he was he was very  critical of the people of his own time  who were skeptical of this who are  saying well you  do philosophy and Latin you know it has  to be done in Greek sort of like we have  people today who say well you're not  really reading Meech if you don't if  you're reading him in translation if you  if you can't use the the German terms  instead of English terms you're not  really understanding it and sister would  say that's bunk the test for whether you  really do understand it can you put it  into your own language and I'm gonna do  that he says and he does do it you know  he he could have accomplished more of  course I think but you know we can say  that about anybody and what he did  accomplish is pretty massive we've lost  a few of his treatises unfortunately and  some of them are fragmentary but what we  do have is a very substantive body of  work that I think is I put it out it's  great because we learn about these these  great schools and what they thought  often down to very detailed analyses and  minutiae but it's also Cicero showing  his own hand a good bit at the time  so let me talk a little bit about his  his eclecticism it's a very reasoned  very measured eclecticism he thinks that  it is important to know the field and  then to select what you think is  actually right he does in fact identify  himself with the academic school but  he's not a rigorous academic skeptic in  the sense of being Orthodox and saying  nothing can really be known or anything  like that he is just as willing to draw  on the Peripatetic or Aristotelian  tradition or the Platonic tradition or  especially the stoic tradition like I  mentioned you know on Duty's is entirely  a work of stoic philosophy with a little  bit of stuff mixed in and in the  Tusculum disputations he actually says  if I was not a member of the school that  I'm a member of I definitely would be a  stoic so he's somebody who is willing to  try to synthesize the best in all of  these different modes of thought which i  think is a very admirable quality it's  not just like eclectic in the sense of  you know there's a nice smorgasbord in  front of me and I'm gonna pick little  bits and pieces and not try to make them  actually match up with each other he is  really thinking through what are the  Stoics right about  where where do things go wrong and he  gives you reasons for this and he has  characters often exchanging and views  and criticizing each other's points of  view  on this very matter so I mean these are  all things that I find just fascinating  about his work the last thing I'll say  is why I mentioned at the beginning that  he's not really held in high esteem even  today and definitely wasn't during the  19th century  why not because they were they were  telling this story of philosophy that  now we know is really not all that true  which is that you know philosophy arises  in the Greek city-states  and it's primarily important in you know  what Plato and Aristotle do and then  with the Stoics and the academics and  and the Epicureans do it's kind of  second-rate it's it's not really as you  know important as what these guys are  doing and then you know people like like  you know Plutarch and Cicero these these  people bringing things together and  eclectic fashions they're really not  that important at all they're not doing  anything original and I think that  that's really bunk I think we have a we  need a different conception of  originality and we need to have  different standards of value I think if  we if we actually adopted a Ciceronian  standard of value we might say I don't I  don't think we would say that he's more  important than Aristotle or Plato but I  think we would say that he really does  deserve to be on the same level I know  that he certainly does for me and I am  looking forward to years and years and  years of having him as a companion as I  read through his works and talk about  them with others and teach them so I  thought I would share some of that with  with you and now you know a little bit  more about my own philosophical  development and commitments at least  with respect to Marcus Tullius Cicero  you
 

